{
  "dom_id": "step-q143677",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/7602d1695610361f28613d8bf3542056b2624f9b6b8980873f6fdebf802c6750.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\frac{2x}{3}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$",
  "worked_solution": "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\frac{2x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{3C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\frac{2x}{3}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/015ca9336646a7545de842414ace2c2a695688e30653b2ed019000d69609d56d.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\frac{2x}{3})$ by $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\u27f9\\,C=(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{2}{3})\\,\u27f9\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$",
